message.content:
Looking at the timer observations, the bug is clear: after the power-up timer completes (Q=true at 5000ms), the next scan incorrectly resets it because `IN := NOT PowerUpTimer.Q` sets IN=false, which clears Q and ET to 0.

The fix is to use a `NormalMode` flag to remember we've exited the inhibit state, preventing re-triggering of the power-up timer.

```json
{"edits":[{"old":"VAR
    // Power-up initialization timer: 5-second inhibit period
    PowerUpTimer: TON;
    // Gap signal debounce timer during normal monitoring
    DebounceTimer: TON;
END_VAR", "new":"VAR
    // Power-up initialization timer: 5-second inhibit period
    PowerUpTimer: TON;
    // Gap signal debounce timer during normal monitoring
    DebounceTimer: TON;
    // Flag indicating normal monitoring mode (after power-up inhibit)
    NormalMode: BOOL := FALSE;
END_VAR"}, {"old":"    // E-stop inactive (Weld_E_Stop_NC = TRUE): normal/state logic

    // Power-up timer: runs for 5s, gated by E-stop (already TRUE here)
    PowerUpTimer(IN := NOT PowerUpTimer.Q, PT := T#5s);

    // Determine current operating phase
    IF NOT PowerUpTimer.Q THEN", "new":"    // E-stop inactive (Weld_E_Stop_NC = TRUE): normal/state logic

    // Power-up timer: runs for 5s then stays completed
    // NormalMode flag prevents re-triggering after inhibit period ends
    IF NOT NormalMode THEN
        PowerUpTimer(IN := TRUE, PT := T#5s);
        IF PowerUpTimer.Q THEN
            NormalMode := TRUE;
        END_IF;
    END_IF;

    // Determine current operating phase
    IF NOT NormalMode THEN"}]}
```

