I need to reconsider the timer logic here. Looking at the execution sequence, the timer state carries forward across calls, so I should verify whether resetting the input each cycle actually maintains the correct behavior or if it causes unintended resets.

In CODESYS, the timer input only triggers a reset when transitioning from true to false—holding it false doesn't continuously reset. Once the power-up timer reaches its threshold and Q becomes true, keeping IN = false maintains that completed state. The issue with the previous implementation was that it re-enabled the timer on the next cycle, causing it to restart rather than stay done. This revised approach keeps the timer in a stable completed state once it finishes.

Looking at the test cases, weld-time-4 shows the system at exactly 5000ms with the timer still running (Q = false), while weld-time-5 should have advanced to 5749ms with the timer now complete. The progression needs to reflect the elapsed time between test points while maintaining the timer's final state. The issue is that `poweruptimer` is being reset to FALSE when it should remain TRUE. Looking at the state transitions, once the inhibit period completes and `NormalMode` becomes TRUE, the power-up timer shouldn't be re-triggered. The fix is to ensure `NormalMode` is set when the timer completes, and then prevent the timer from being reset on subsequent cycles.

The solution involves adding a `NormalMode` flag that tracks whether we've exited the inhibit state. When `poweruptimer.Q` becomes TRUE, set `NormalMode := TRUE` to lock in that state. Then use this flag to control the power-up timer input—only enable it if we haven't yet entered normal mode. This way, once normal operation begins, the power-up timer stays inactive and won't interfere with the debounce logic. I need to check the test case for weld-time-7 to understand the E-stop behavior. With E-stop active (Weld_E_Stop_NC = false), the expected outputs show Operator_Alert = false and Weld_Soft_Start_Active = false, which aligns with the implementation since Operator_Alert is set to false in the E-stop branch and Weld_Soft_Start_Active gets set to false when E-stop is inactive.

The problem appears to be isolated to weld-time-5 and weld-time-6, where the system remains in power-up mode when it should transition to normal mode. Adding a NormalMode flag and adjusting the power-up timer logic should resolve this timing issue.

